          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve describes **metformin**, taken together with structured lifestyle changes, as the usual starting drug for type 2 diabetes when no other serious condition is present. This comes from the National diabetes association standards of care summary (guideline-type-2-diabetes-first-line-therapy). The same summary says the older rule of \"metformin first, then add drugs in order as control worsens\" has been replaced. Current guidance picks the first drug based on other conditions a person has, and for several of those conditions the choice does not depend on the HbA1c blood test result.\n\nThe passage I retrieved does not list which drugs apply to which conditions, so I can't tell you those choices from these sources. Two related points came up in other documents:\n\n- A heart-failure guideline lists an SGLT-2 inhibitor as a foundational drug class for reduced-ejection-fraction heart failure, whether or not diabetes is present. That is a heart-failure recommendation, not a diabetes first-line rule.\n- A blood-pressure guideline says renin-angiotensin blockers are preferred where diabetes comes with albuminuria (protein in the urine) or chronic kidney disease. That concerns blood pressure treatment, not glucose-lowering drugs.\n\nOn differences between authorities: I found only one source that addresses first-line diabetes drugs, so I cannot report a disagreement on this question. A glycemic-targets document does note a disagreement section, but I did not retrieve its text, so I can't say what it contains.\n\nWhether metformin or another drug suits a particular person is a decision for their clinician, who can take their kidney function, heart history and other conditions into account.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
